===== gwi gmail account import-legacy =====
Migrates today's legacy (profile/base `env`) Gmail credentials into a named account

Usage: gwi gmail account import-legacy [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see [ADR-0066](../../../docs/adrs/adr-0066.md)). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --name <NAME>
          Name to migrate the legacy credentials into.
          
          Deliberately `--name`, not `--account`: the global `--account` flag is inherited by every subcommand (clap rejects two args bound to the same long flag on one command), and it means something different here anyway — this names the account being *created*, not one being selected.
          
          [default: default]

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --remove-legacy
          Also remove the legacy credentials after a successful migration

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail search =====
Searches Gmail messages (mirrors the `gmail_search` MCP tool)

Usage: gwi gmail search [OPTIONS] --query <QUERY>

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see [ADR-0066](../../../docs/adrs/adr-0066.md)). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --query <QUERY>
          Gmail search query (same syntax as the Gmail search box, e.g. `label:finance after:2026/01/01`)

      --limit <LIMIT>
          Maximum results to return. `0` means "fetch every match" (capped at a hard ceiling to bound run time and quota)
          
          [default: 50]

      --enrich
          Enrich each hit with From/Subject/Date/snippet via one extra `messages.get` request per hit. Without this flag, `search` returns only `id`/`threadId` — the cheaper default. Combined with `--limit 0` this can issue thousands of requests; use deliberately

      --concurrency <CONCURRENCY>
          Bounds concurrent `messages.get` calls when `--enrich` is set (has no effect otherwise). Clamped to 1..=5, based on a 100-units/second pacing budget and 20 units per get. Bounds fan-out, not request rate
          
          [default: 4]

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail read =====
Reads a single Gmail message (mirrors the `gmail_message_read` MCP tool)

Usage: gwi gmail read [OPTIONS] <MESSAGE_ID>

Arguments:
  <MESSAGE_ID>
          Gmail message id

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see [ADR-0066](../../../docs/adrs/adr-0066.md)). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --out-file <PATH>
          Output file (writes to stdout if omitted). With `--detail raw`, the message's exact RFC 2822 bytes, i.e. an `.eml` file

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --detail <DETAIL>
          How much of the message to fetch

          Possible values:
          - minimal:  Only `id`/`threadId`/`labelIds`/`sizeEstimate` — no headers or body
          - metadata: Headers and snippet only, no body
          - full:     The full parsed MIME structure. Default
          - raw:      The full RFC 2822 message, base64url-encoded
          
          [default: full]

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table:    Human-readable table (id/thread-id/labels/snippet). Default
          - json:     JSON
          - yaml:     YAML (single document)
          - yamls:    YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl:    JSON Lines
          - markdown: Human-readable Markdown rendering of the full message (headers + body). Always fetches the complete raw MIME message regardless of `--detail`, since rendering needs the full message structure
          
          [default: table]

      --fold-quotes
          Collapses `>`-quoted reply history nested more than one level deep into a one-line `*(N quoted lines omitted)*` marker (#1514). Only affects `-o markdown`, mirroring `--detail`'s reverse asymmetry (it is silently ignored elsewhere). Off by default: verbatim rendering is fully information-preserving, and the full text is one re-render away without this flag

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

